Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.670 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  ten years ago I worked with my friend  Luke on the hot dusty plains of West  Texas I recently reconnected with Luke  and when I did he shared with me an  unsettling story when Luke was in  college and recently married he came  across an abandoned hunting rifle which  he dutifully took down to the local  police station to turn it while he was  there he was told that the rifle had  been reported missing and that he was  under arrest for possession of a stolen  firearm a serious felony Luke spent the  next six days in jail on charges that  were ultimately dropped and for the  first three days his wife had no idea  where he was because she had an  out-of-state cellphone number and a jail  wouldn't allow long-distance calls  so what was of the kept Luke in jail not  just overnight but for six days it was  bail set at $100,000 now if your college  jobs were at all like mine which at one  point included Campus Bookstore return  specialist $100,000 was just way beyond  out of reach and it was relieved too but  bail is rigid it isn't means-tested it's  an old system that needs reform to  better align with our values we live in  a society that values the promise of  liberty and justice for all  but our pretrial justice system is only  delivering liberty and justice for some  so today can we do three things together  first let's take a walk into this  pretrial justice system something that  you may never may have never thought  about and until recently I hadn't either  second let's consider if it matters  whether someone spends the time between  their arrest and their trial in jail  and third let's look at way as the Uni  will interact with the system going  forward before we get going I just feel  an obligation to acknowledge that I am  not the typical person to address a  topic like this I've had a lucky  upbringing nobody in my family has spent  time in jail but it makes me angry to  think about Luke and his wife separated  for six days and it isn't just Luke  every night in the u.s. seven hundred  thousand people sit in local jails sixty  percent of them are there just waiting  for their trial so to walk into this  system after arrest everyone will face  one of three doors so door number one  release this is where people who are low  risk not too much of a danger to the  community and are likely to show up to  all of their court dates they're free to  go until the time of their trial door  number two detention this is where  people who are high risk they may not be  likely to show up to all of their court  dates or they may have been charged with  a particularly egregious offence and so  they'll stay in jail until the time of  their child door number three bail this  is the door that most people have to  walk through bail requires that someone  deposit money with the court that they  will only get back even when they show  up for all of their court dates and if  you think about it that's kind of a good  system in principle I mean we want some  sort of incentive for people to show up  for court but when you dig into the data  it's clear that this is a tricky door to  have to walk through you see in San Jose  two years ago the average bail amount  was twenty seven thousand dollars twenty  seven thousand dollars is enough to buy  a brand-new Toyota Camry with leather  seats or make a down payment on a house  anywhere outside of the Bay Area  so this is like a lot of money and to me  this is compelling evidence that our  pretrial justice system keeps people in  jail simply based on who can and who  cannot pay so on the one hand you people  with means who can pay their way out of  jail regardless of their risk to society  and on the other hand your people like  Luke  people who are stuck behind bars simply  because they can't pay now to topic  number two doesn't matter if someone  spends this time between arrest and  trial in jail or not if someone is  unable to pay bail they can wait for  weeks or months for that trial and over  the course of that time at risk of  losing a job a relationship with a  significant other and if and when they  do get out it is that much harder to put  food on the table or pay rent now it  isn't just the sort of run-of-the-mill  everyday life stuff that gets it changes  Brooklyn defender services look at two  groups of people that they serve in New  York City group number one these are  folks who stayed in jail between the  time of their arrest and trial and group  number two these are people who got out  you know what they found  group number one was five times more  likely to end up with a criminal  conviction five times and you know what  contributes to this it's the fact that  when you're in jail it's hard to meet  with your lawyer it's hard to build your  case and some people will plead guilty  simply to move the process along but  that criminal conviction follows them  when they apply for jobs in the future  or try to rent an apartment and so the  bottom line here is that getting out  really matters to illustrate this I'd  like to share the story of kalief  Browder and some of you may be familiar  with his tragic tale Khalif was a 16  year old living in New York City when he  was arrested for allegedly stealing a  backpack his bail was set at only three  thousand dollars but his family couldn't  afford that  Khalif spent the next three years on  Rikers Island a notorious jail facility  in New York at the end of those three  years his charges were dropped  and he wasn't convicted of anything and  sadly just a couple years after his  release  Calise committed suicide and many point  to the mental and emotional trauma of  his time in jail as a major contributing  factor so how does this happen I mean  this is insane  three thousand dollars cost him three  years and his life Khalif didn't have  his Liberty and as a result he lost his  shot at justice so again the bottom line  here is the getting out really matters  how are you and I going to interact with  the system going forward now we may  never have to walk through any one of  these three doors but you're going to  read articles about this topic online  and you'll be asked to vote on criminal  justice reform issues in your home state  I am NOT the only person talking about  this John Oliver did a spot on bail the  New York Times and NPR are writing about  it and jay-z actually just produced a  mini-series on the kalief Browder story  so read these articles and get informed  because you will be asked to weigh in  I'd like to share two examples of this  in 2014 New Jersey passed a ballot  measure that did away with their old  money bail system and it replaced it  with a RISC algorithm now what this does  is it scores every defendant on a scale  from one to six and it's based on their  likelihood of not showing up to court  and their risk to the community so this  score based on their actual risk is what  determines whether they stay in jail or  not it's not their ability to pay and  while these algorithms are not perfect  by any means they're an important step  forward in the right direction  a little bit closer to home there's  actually a bill working its way through  the California Legislature currently it  would have similar effects so the  this is relevant for us right now it  would shift people from door number  three Bale to door number one release  now for the last year I've been working  with a team of students here at Stanford  to find business based solutions to this  problem and if this is at all  interesting to you I have all ears  please come talk to me  smart interested folks like yourselves  are critical for coming up with a better  system  so I want you to leave here today  knowing that this matters this matters  to the Luc's it matters to the khalif's  and it matters to thousands of others  who are trapped in our pretrial justice  system so let's renew a commitment to  our values and let's take an important  step forward in delivering on the  promise of liberty and justice not just  for some but liberty and justice for all  [Applause]  [Music]

